# StudySphere — GitHub Repository Guide

## What Else Is Needed in Each Repository

Beyond `README.md`, a well-structured GitHub repository should include the following files. Some you already have (`.gitignore`, `build.gradle`); the rest should be added.

---

### 1. `LICENSE`
> [!IMPORTANT]
> GitHub will show a **"No license"** warning on your repo without this. It also protects you legally.

For a student project with open-source intent, use **MIT**:

```
MIT License

Copyright (c) 2026 MenaraPereraa

Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy
of this software and associated documentation files (the "Software"), to deal
in the Software without restriction, including without limitation the rights
to use, copy, modify, merge, publish, distribute, sublicense, and/or sell
copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the Software is
furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:

The above copyright notice and this permission notice shall be included in all
copies or substantial portions of the Software.

THE SOFTWARE IS PROVIDED "AS IS", WITHOUT WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS OR
IMPLIED, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE WARRANTIES OF MERCHANTABILITY,
FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE AND NONINFRINGEMENT. IN NO EVENT SHALL THE
AUTHORS OR COPYRIGHT HOLDERS BE LIABLE FOR ANY CLAIM, DAMAGES OR OTHER
LIABILITY, WHETHER IN AN ACTION OF CONTRACT, TORT OR OTHERWISE, ARISING FROM,
OUT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SOFTWARE OR THE USE OR OTHER DEALINGS IN THE
SOFTWARE.
```

**How to add it:**
```bash
# Add it directly on GitHub via the UI:
# Repository → Add file → Create new file → name it "LICENSE"
# GitHub will offer a license template picker automatically.
```

---

### 2. `.gitignore` (already present — verify contents)

Your `.gitignore` should exclude all secrets and generated files. For an Android Java + Firebase project:

```gitignore
# Android generated files
*.iml
.gradle/
local.properties
.idea/
build/
captures/
.externalNativeBuild/
.cxx/
*.apk
*.aab
*.ap_

# Firebase — NEVER commit this
google-services.json

# Keystore — NEVER commit signing keys
*.jks
*.keystore

# OS files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Logs
*.log
```

---

### 3. `CONTRIBUTING.md`
Explains how others (or teammates) can contribute. Keeps collaboration clean.

```markdown
# Contributing to StudySphere

Thank you for your interest in contributing!

## How to Contribute

1. Fork the repository
2. Create a feature branch: `git checkout -b feature/your-feature-name`
3. Commit your changes: `git commit -m "feat: add your feature description"`
4. Push to your fork: `git push origin feature/your-feature-name`
5. Open a Pull Request against the `main` branch

## Commit Message Convention

Use the following prefixes:
- `feat:` — new feature
- `fix:` — bug fix
- `docs:` — documentation changes
- `refactor:` — code restructuring without feature change
- `test:` — adding or updating tests

## Code Style

- Follow standard Android Java naming conventions
- Use 4-space indentation
- Add Javadoc comments to all public methods
```

---

### 4. `.github/` Folder (GitHub-specific automation)

This folder unlocks GitHub's built-in features. Create this structure:

```
.github/
├── ISSUE_TEMPLATE/
│   ├── bug_report.md          # Pre-filled template when someone opens a bug
│   └── feature_request.md     # Pre-filled template for feature requests
├── PULL_REQUEST_TEMPLATE.md   # Checklist shown when a PR is opened
└── workflows/
    └── android-ci.yml         # GitHub Actions: auto-build on every push
```

**`android-ci.yml`** — Automatically builds your app on every push to `main`:

```yaml
name: Android CI

on:
  push:
    branches: [ "main" ]
  pull_request:
    branches: [ "main" ]

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up JDK 17
        uses: actions/setup-java@v4
        with:
          java-version: '17'
          distribution: 'temurin'

      - name: Grant execute permission for gradlew
        run: chmod +x gradlew

      - name: Build Debug APK
        run: ./gradlew assembleDebug

      - name: Run Unit Tests
        run: ./gradlew test
```

---

### 5. `CHANGELOG.md` *(Optional but professional)*

Tracks version history:

```markdown
# Changelog

## [Unreleased]
- Initial project setup
- Firebase Authentication integration
- Marketplace listing feature

## [1.0.0] - 2026-10-18
- First public release
```

---

## How to Add These Files to Git

```bash
# Stage all new files
git add LICENSE CONTRIBUTING.md CHANGELOG.md
git add .gitignore
git add .github/

# Commit them together
git commit -m "docs: add LICENSE, CONTRIBUTING, CHANGELOG, and GitHub Actions CI"

# Push to GitHub
git push origin main
```

---

## Do You Need Separate README Files for Separate Repos?

**Yes — absolutely.** Each repository is its own independent project on GitHub. GitHub only reads the `README.md` at the **root of that specific repo**. A README in one repo has no effect on another.

Here is the recommended split for StudySphere:

---

## Recommended Repository Structure

### Repository 1 — `StudySphere` *(Android App — already exists)*
> The mobile client. Java + Android SDK + Firebase + PayHere SDK + Google Maps.

```
MenaraPereraa/StudySphere
├── app/
├── gradle/
├── .gitignore              ✅ already present
├── README.md               ✅ already created
├── LICENSE                 ← add this
├── CONTRIBUTING.md         ← add this
├── CHANGELOG.md            ← add this (optional)
├── .github/workflows/      ← add CI
├── build.gradle
├── settings.gradle
├── gradle.properties
├── gradlew
└── gradlew.bat
```

---

### Repository 2 — `StudySphere-Backend`
> The REST API server. Recommended: **Node.js (Express)** or **Spring Boot (Java)**.
> Handles business logic, PayHere payment webhooks, and any server-side operations.

```
MenaraPereraa/StudySphere-Backend
├── src/
│   ├── controllers/
│   ├── routes/
│   ├── models/
│   ├── middleware/
│   └── index.js
├── .env.example            ← template for environment variables (safe to commit)
├── .gitignore              ← exclude .env, node_modules/
├── README.md               ← separate README for this repo
├── LICENSE
├── CONTRIBUTING.md
├── package.json
└── .github/workflows/
```

**Its README should cover:**
- API base URL and endpoints
- Environment variables (`.env.example`)
- How to run locally (`npm start` / `mvn spring-boot:run`)
- PayHere webhook setup
- Deployment (Railway / Render / AWS)

---

### Repository 3 — `StudySphere-Web` *(if applicable)*
> Admin panel or web version. Recommended: **Next.js / React**.
> Could serve as a seller dashboard or admin management panel.

```
MenaraPereraa/StudySphere-Web
├── src/
│   ├── app/
│   ├── components/
│   └── lib/
├── .env.example
├── .gitignore
├── README.md               ← separate README for this repo
├── LICENSE
├── CONTRIBUTING.md
├── package.json
├── next.config.js
└── .github/workflows/
```

**Its README should cover:**
- Setup with `npm install` and `npm run dev`
- Environment variables (Firebase config, API base URL)
- Deployment to Vercel

---

## Summary Checklist

| File / Folder | Android Repo | Backend Repo | Web Repo |
|---|:---:|:---:|:---:|
| `README.md` | ✅ done | needed | needed |
| `LICENSE` | add | add | add |
| `.gitignore` | ✅ present | add | add |
| `CONTRIBUTING.md` | add | add | add |
| `.env.example` | — | add | add |
| `.github/workflows/` CI | add | add | add |
| `CHANGELOG.md` | optional | optional | optional |

> [!TIP]
> Create the README first in every new repo — GitHub shows a prompt to add it during repo creation. Say **yes** and then replace the content with your custom file.

